Public sitemap entry points are:

| URL | Route entry | Inventory |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `/sitemap.xml` | `src/app/sitemap.ts` | Stable public routes |
| `/agents/sitemap.xml` | `src/app/(client)/agents/sitemap.ts` | Agent profiles |
| `/blog/sitemap.xml` | `src/app/(client)/blog/sitemap.ts` | DatoCMS articles |
| `/properties/sitemap.xml` | `src/app/(client)/properties/sitemap.ts` | `/properties` plus one canonical `id-title` URL per property |
| `/properties/filter/sitemap.xml` | `src/app/(client)/properties/filter/sitemap.xml/route.ts` | Sitemap index for navigation-backed filter chunks |

Filter child URL sets are served by
`src/app/(client)/properties/filter/sitemaps/[chunk]/route.ts`.

The filter sitemap URL is an XML sitemap index whose child URL sets are capped
at 1,900,000 UTF-8 bytes. A 24-hour manifest caches one consistent snapshot of
real, navigation-backed property filter prefixes and their chunk boundaries;
each child generates only its assigned range. Query strings and pagination are
excluded from sitemap inventory. The property sitemap paginates through the API
but publishes only canonical property-detail paths, not listing pages. Other
sitemap routes fetch external data during static page generation, so a
production build needs valid environment variables and reachable REST/DatoCMS
endpoints. `public/robots.txt` advertises all five sitemap entry points.

## Property domain

Properties are the largest feature area.

### Core contracts

- `src/lib/types/properties.ts` defines `Property`, property enums,
  specifications, facilities, configuration, navigation, images, currency,
  and rent-time contracts.
- `src/lib/types/join.ts` defines `PropertyJoinAgent` as the tuple
  `[Property, Agent]`. Index `0` is always the property and index `1` is the
  assigned agent.
- `src/lib/types/json-response.ts` defines the backend envelopes:
  `JsonResponse<T>` and `DataAndPagination<T>`.

### API and query layer

- `src/lib/api/properties.ts` is the canonical property API module. It owns
  `FindPropertyQuery`, CRUD payloads, public listing/detail/navigation calls,
  and protected create/update/delete calls.
- `src/lib/hooks/properties.ts` is the canonical React Query module. It owns
  `propertyKeys`, query option factories, and mutation option factories.
- Use barrel imports from `@/lib/api`, `@/lib/hooks`, and `@/lib/types` unless
  a direct module import materially improves clarity.

Primary REST endpoints:

| API function | HTTP endpoint |
| --- | --- |
| `findUniquePropertyJoinAgent(id)` | `GET /properties/{id}/join-agents` |
| `findPropertyJoinAgent(query)` | `GET /properties/join-agents?...` |
| `findPropertySitePaths()` | `GET /properties/site-paths` |
| `findPropertyNavigation()` | `GET /properties/navigations` |
| `createProperty(payload)` | `POST /properties` |
| `updateProperty(id, payload)` | `PUT /properties/{id}` |
| `removeProperty(id)` | `DELETE /properties/{id}` |

`FindPropertyQuery` supports ID/IDs, agent, location, purchase/sold status,
building type/condition, keyword, popular/prime/related flags, pagination, and
price sorting.

### UI boundaries

- `src/app/(client)/properties/_components` contains listing/card/filter
  components shared across the property, agent, blog, bookmark, and comparison
  experiences.
- `src/app/(client)/properties/[id]/_components` contains detail-only UI.
- `src/app/(client)/properties/_components/fillters` is the existing directory
  name for property filters; preserve it unless performing a deliberate rename
  across all imports.
- `src/app/(client)/properties/_lib/parse-filter-params.ts` converts SEO path
  segments into API query parameters.
- `src/lib/metadata/seo-domain.ts` owns the canonical `id-title` property-path
  builder used by cards, metadata, sitemaps, and share/contact URLs.
- Filter route metadata validates indexable path prefixes against the cached
  navigation-backed sitemap manifest; query variants and unknown paths remain
  crawlable but receive `noindex, follow`.
- `src/app/(client)/properties/_lib/bookmark-property.ts` stores bookmark IDs
  under `bookmark_ids` in browser `localStorage`.
- `src/lib/hooks/local-storage.ts` exposes bookmarks as TanStack Query options
  so cards and comparison views can refetch after local changes.
- `src/app/(admin)/admin/properties` owns the URL-driven management table. Its
  server page normalizes supported search parameters before the client table
  applies React Query fetching and Admin/Agent visibility scoping.
- The admin route keeps filters and table columns in separate `_components`
  folders. Route-local `_lib` helpers derive independent select options from
  site paths and coordinate pagination/filter URL updates; focused `node:test`
  coverage lives beside those helpers.
- Property create and edit routes share the route-level `PropertyForm`, which
  uses React Hook Form/Zod section cards, navigation-backed creatable location
  fields, S3 image uploads, and Admin-only popular/sold-channel controls.
- `src/app/(admin)/admin/properties/_lib/property-form-domain.ts` is the pure
  form-domain boundary for defaults, validation, navigation options, compact
  prices, safe Google Maps embeds, cover-image invariants, and API payload
  conversion. Its focused `node:test` coverage lives beside it.
- Property table actions link to edit and confirm deletion before calling the
  protected property mutation; successful CRUD operations invalidate the full
  property query-key family, including navigation and detail data.

## Other domains

### Agents

- Contract: `src/lib/types/agent.ts`
- REST API: `src/lib/api/agents.ts`
- Query/mutation options: `src/lib/hooks/agents.ts`
- Public route UI: `src/app/(client)/agents`
- Agent profile content remains client-rendered. After its query resolves it
  emits `ProfilePage`/`Person` JSON-LD using the canonical profile URL.
- Admin CRUD UI: `src/app/(admin)/admin/agents`; create and edit share the
  route-level `AgentForm`, while edit loads by ID and refreshes agent query
  caches after saving. The list and create pages require the Admin role, while
  agents retain access to their own dynamic profile route.
- Agent profile pictures are uploaded through the S3 API before create or
  picture-changing update requests.
- Protected operations send the `accessToken` cookie as `x-access-token`.

### Blog/articles

- Contract: `src/lib/types/article.ts`
- GraphQL operations: `src/lib/api/gql.ts`
- Apollo client and fetch functions: `src/lib/api/articles.ts`
- Routes and schema builders: `src/app/(client)/blog`
- Data source: DatoCMS using `DATOCMS_API_URL` and `DATOCMS_API_TOKEN`.
- Article pages emit synchronous `Article` and Home -> Blog -> Article
  breadcrumb JSON-LD; related-content cards are not represented as breadcrumb
  nodes.

### Developers

- Contract: `src/lib/types/developers.ts`
- REST API: `src/lib/api/developers.ts`
- Query/mutation options: `src/lib/hooks/developers.ts`
- Admin CRUD UI: `src/app/(admin)/admin/developers`; create and edit share
  the route-level `DeveloperForm`, while edit retains the current logo unless a
  replacement is uploaded to S3. A route layout requires the Admin role for
  the entire developer subtree.
- The home page renders developer logos in the partners section.

### Leads

- Contract: `src/lib/types/leads.ts`
- REST API: `src/lib/api/leads.ts`
- Query/mutation options: `src/lib/hooks/leads.ts`
- `FindLeadQuery` supports optional `agent_id` filtering, and lead-list query
  keys include the filter so admin and agent results remain cache-isolated.
- Admin read-only UI: `src/app/(admin)/admin/leads`; the shared sidebar exposes
  it to admins and agents, and agent requests include `agent_id`.
- `src/app/(client)/properties/_components/contact-agent-dialog.tsx`
  validates contact details, creates a lead, reports analytics events, and then
  opens WhatsApp or the phone dialer.

### S3 images

- Contract: `src/lib/types/s3.ts`
- Client upload API: `src/lib/api/s3.ts`
- Mutation options: `src/lib/hooks/s3.ts`
- Allowed upload formats are PNG, JPEG/JPG, and WebP.

## Data and rendering conventions

### REST

`src/lib/api/fetch-api.ts` prefixes endpoints with `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL`, sends
JSON by default, parses the JSON response, and throws on non-2xx responses.
It is intentionally usable from both server and client module graphs.

Typical paginated response access is:

```ts
response.data?.data       // domain records
response.data?.pagination // page, per_page, total_pages, total
```

### Authentication

`src/lib/api/token.ts` is a server function that reads the `accessToken`
cookie. Protected REST calls place the value in the `x-access-token` header.
Admin-only page guards use `src/lib/api/verified-viewer.ts` to verify the token
with the session API before trusting its viewer role. Pure claim validation and
Admin/Agent role checks live in `src/lib/api/viewer-session.ts`.

### Client state

TanStack Query is the primary server-state mechanism. Each domain hook module
owns its query-key factory and its `queryOptions`/`mutationOptions` builders.
Component-local UI state uses React state; bookmarked IDs use localStorage.

### SEO

- `src/lib/metadata/create-metadata.ts` builds canonical, Open Graph, Twitter,
  image, locale, site-name, and explicit robots metadata from a shared
  `{ title, description, path, image?, index? }` contract.
- Pure SEO path, text, social metadata, and robots policies:
  `src/lib/metadata/seo-domain.ts`
- Shared JSON-LD: `src/lib/schema`
- Route-specific metadata/schema helpers live beside their route under `_lib`.
- JSON-LD scripts stringify data and escape `<` as `\u003c` before insertion.
- `public/llms.txt` identifies citation-ready public resources, while
  `public/robots.txt` states explicit access rules for major AI crawlers.
- `public/property-catalog.md` is a committed, citation-friendly static property
  snapshot; it does not change page rendering. The generator that originally
  produced it is no longer present in `scripts`.
- Primary listing pages use `CollectionPage`/`ItemList`; property details use
  `RealEstateListing`, `Place`, `Offer`, and canonical breadcrumbs. Organization
  and website schema are emitted from the public shell/home schema boundary.
  Visible FAQ copy and `FAQPage` schema share the plain data in
  `src/app/(client)/properties/_components/faq/faq-content.ts`.
- Query-filtered listings, invalid filters, comparison/bookmark utilities, and
  missing resources use `noindex, follow`; authentication and admin routes use
  `noindex, nofollow`.
- Focused SEO regression tests live in
  `src/lib/metadata/seo-domain.test.cjs`, `src/lib/schema/seo-schema.test.cjs`,
  `src/lib/metadata/ai-discovery.test.cjs`,
  `src/lib/schema/faq-schema.test.cjs`, and
  `src/app/(client)/properties/filter/_lib/property-filter-sitemap.test.cjs`.

## Shared UI

- Reuse primitives from `src/components/ui` before introducing a new base
  component.
- Product-level shared components live in `src/components/custom-ui`.
- Route-specific components stay next to their route in `_components`.
- `src/lib/utils.ts` exports the shared `cn` class-name helper.
- Tailwind CSS provides styling; `src/app/globals.css` owns global tokens and
  styles.

## Environment variables

Validation is centralized in `src/lib/env.ts` with `@t3-oss/env-nextjs` and
Zod.

Server-only:

- `SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN`
- `DATOCMS_API_URL`
- `DATOCMS_API_TOKEN`

Client and server:

- `NEXT_PUBLIC_HOST_URL`
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL`
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_S3_ENDPOINT`
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN`
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_ID`

Even variables whose schema accepts an empty string must be present. The Docker
builder declares all of these as build arguments because Next.js evaluates
environment validation and prerendered routes during `yarn build`.

## Observability and deployment

- `src/instrumentation.ts`, `instrumentation-client.ts`,
  `sentry.server.config.ts`, and `sentry.edge.config.ts` configure Sentry.
- `next.config.mjs` wraps the app with Sentry configuration and produces a
  standalone Next.js output.
- The Docker build uses Node 22 Alpine and copies `.next/standalone` plus static
  assets into the runtime image.
- The development server and Docker Compose host binding use port `4137`; the
  container listens on `3000`.

